deep.reconstruction:
Setting: L is a totally ordered set (a 'ligne') with its order topology. It possibly has a smallest element or(L) and a largest element ex(L). The previous page introduced the closed sub-tronçons: [a,b] (a<b), L_{<=a}, L_{>=a}, L itself, and singletons. This page sets up the boundary bookkeeping for intervals, part of a combinatorial-topological set-up for 'réseaux via découpages' (networks defined by cutting lines into intervals).

(1) Absolute boundary. For a closed sub-tronçon T, the 'bord absolu' is the set of its extremities, i.e. its least and greatest elements when they exist:
- ∂{a} = {a};
- ∂[a,b] = {a,b} for a<b;
- ∂L_{>=a} = ∂L_{<=a} = {a}, with a marginal proviso (roughly 'if a is not in ∂L' or 'considered in L');
- ∂L = ∂L 'sic', a consistency remark: the absolute boundary of L is {or(L), ex(L)} ∩ L.

(2) Comparison with the topological frontier. Write Ṫ for the frontier of T in L. Then Ṫ∩T ⊂ ∂T ⊂ (Ṫ∩T) ∪ ∂L. In words: a point of T on the frontier is an extremity of T, and an extremity of T is on the frontier unless it is an extremity of L itself. Grothendieck sharpens this to the boxed formula ∂T = (Ṫ∩T) ⊔ (T∩∂L). He crossed out a separate treatment of the case card T = 1.

(3) Tronçons in general. These are the sets T' = T∖α with T a closed tronçon and α ⊂ ∂T. They are exactly: ]a,b], [a,b[, ]a,b[ (a<b); L_{<a} (a ≠ or L); L_{>a} (a ≠ ex L); and L. A marginal NB states that this writing is unique when T ≠ T'. Presumably T is recovered as the closure of T' and α as T∖T'.

The next page names these open and semi-open tronçons. It then defines 'position ordinale modulaire' of two tronçons via total orderedness of ∂T ∪ ∂T'. This is all preliminary vocabulary for cutting lines into pieces in the 'réseaux' construction.
